{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 See how your instrument's sound unfolds in relation to MIDI notes.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b ALPHA}: parameters, file layout and saved-project compatibility may change without a migration path. Try a disposable project before relying on it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 BloomScope visualizes the amplitude envelope of a sample-based instrument's combined audio output over time using Peak and RMS traces. Place it after the instrument to compare that sound with an optional MIDI note strip, from attack through sustain and release.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Chords, ensembles, overlapping notes and release tails contribute to the measured signal. This is not a separate envelope for each MIDI note: the detectors measure the resulting audio, not a sum of per-note envelopes. Received note-on/off events are timing references, not isolated voice levels.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 MIDI input is optional. Recorded audio, microphone signals, other instruments, buses and the master mix can also be inspected without MIDI, during playback. Capture runs only while playing, not while stopped, paused or recording. Use project playrate 1 for timing interpretation. Only audio channels 1/2 and bus-0 MIDI note references are analyzed; downstream MIDI delivery depends on the instrument's MIDI-output settings.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Standalone capture has a known first-note timing defect at intra-block loop wraps. The included optional Main action supplies instance-bound loop context; run it explicitly on one selected track containing one BloomScope. Restart it after recompilation. It supports top-level normal-track FX only, unit playrate and loops longer than one audio block. It does not repair earlier history. No automatic startup; audio-device configuration is never changed by it.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Draws the amplitude envelope of the audio at its point in the FX chain: project time across, dBFS up a single axis whose top edge is 0 dB, so the shape of an attack or a release is read instead of guessed. Peak and RMS are extracted per hop of 32 samples, independent of the audio buffer, so detector arithmetic does not depend on block partitioning. Peak is the filled area, RMS the line on top; the trace breaks where the transport jumped, because a locate is not a level. Freeze holds the instant while the audio keeps passing; the status line says what is being shown and that the reading is taken before the track fader.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Optional bus-0 MIDI notes appear below all three audio views, with shared note-on/off guides, readable pitch labels and channel/velocity tooltips. Full/Levels/Off grids and contextual control help are available. Received MIDI passes unchanged. No CC lanes or per-note metrics. Not a loudness meter or an oscilloscope: no K-weighting, no LUFS, no waveform.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Shows what a MIDI controller actually sends: the shape of the movement over time, one fader bar per active CC, and how fast and how regularly each CC transmits, on each MIDI channel separately.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Two display modes: a scrolling time graph with a labelled axis (1-30 s window) and one bar per active CC with its current value. The graph is literal: every received event has a circle marker in all three DOTS modes, even in dense streams. The line between two events is the value the controller HOLDS, drawn as a step -- never an interpolation through values that were never sent. After the last event the held value continues dashed, because there it is an inference rather than a measurement. The same CC on different channels is a separate row, with the same hue and a different shade, and the CC and channel filters combine. Three numbers per row are measured over exactly the last second: Messages (how often the controller sends, including repeats), Motion (absolute MIDI value travel, in CC units/s) and Timing (raw message-interval p50/p95 in ms, with interval count n). Resting on a visible metric explains its reading and descriptive scale; the row label explains CC/channel/color and the colored bar/value its 0-127 range. Timing keeps zeros and long gaps; help adds min/max, coincident timestamp percentage and secondary gap-filtered jitter. Quantiles use nearest rank: sorted rank ceil(p*n). Both endpoints must be inside [now-1s, now]; negative timestamps/out-of-window events break adjacency, backwards intervals are skipped with the current event as next endpoint. Timestamp zero is valid. No interval means n/a; one is shown, but small n is weak evidence. Coincident means equal host timestamps, not physical simultaneity. Secondary jitter is population SD after keeping intervals <=4x raw minimum, needs 4 intervals; a zero minimum keeps only zeros, even when raw intervals include long gaps. Metrics use one second independent of WIN; Freeze keeps the displayed instant.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The pair list sits below the plot or beside it, for windows that are wide and short. Freeze stops the acquisition while MIDI keeps passing through; Clear wipes history and metrics. Colour and name of every CC come from ccscope_cc_table.jsfx-inc -- the file you edit; there is no palette to pick, and the channels of one CC stay four shades of its colour. The parameters form a control bar instead of REAPER sliders and stay automatable, in presets and under Param.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 These describe MIDI output, not physical speed, value resolution or hardware quality. Timing is not latency or a guaranteed bound; no data is not stability. Written for REAPER 6.37 or newer.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Bugs, defined changes and the roadmap live in the repository's issues and discussions, not in the package.\par}
}

{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 \fswiss Helvetica;}{\f1 \fmodern Courier;}}
{\colortbl;\red255\green0\blue0;\red0\green0\blue255;}
\widowctrl\hyphauto

{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Turns the notes you hold into trills. One key passes through, two alternate, three give a sustained drone plus a trill, four give two parallel trills -- each voice on the MIDI channel it was played on.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Rate is in notes per second and does not follow the tempo: a trill is a gesture, not a metrical value. Humanize makes the alternation breathe, Balance gives one of the two notes a little more time, and Ramp Up / Ramp Down let the trill lean in and die away.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The defaults are not a taste: they come from measuring recorded piano performances -- about 12 notes a second, an alternation that is uneven by roughly 20%, and every note ending slightly {\i before} the next attack instead of on top of it. The corpus, the instrument that measures it, the method and the reference values are published in corpus/.\par}
}
